{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"05","chapter_title":"Resume, Branding, and AI Brainstorming","title":"AI Feedback You Should Reject","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting ai feedback you should reject to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-01","statement":"Apply the defensibility test to decide whether to keep or reject an AI resume suggestion"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-02","statement":"Classify a failed AI suggestion as fabrication, inflation, keyword stuffing, or identity guessing"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-03","statement":"Audit a whole-document AI rewrite line by line and log each rejection with its reason in the critique log"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-04","statement":"Explain the asymmetric-cost rule: default to leaving out a flattering claim you could not defend"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming","cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/02/04"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_calibration","cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_identity_guessing"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_calibration","cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_identity_guessing"],"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_calibration","cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# AI Feedback You Should Reject\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting AI Feedback You Should Reject, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/02-05-ai-feedback-you-should-reject.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for AI Feedback You Should Reject: AI Feedback You Should Reject, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAI writing tools are fluent and confident, and that combination is exactly what makes them dangerous for a resume. A tool will happily suggest you add a leadership award you never won, inflate a GPA, or stuff a keyword to \"beat the applicant tracking system,\" and it will phrase the suggestion as helpful expertise. The earlier sections taught you to use AI critique; this one teaches the inverse and more important skill, recognizing which AI suggestions to refuse. \"The AI said so\" is never a defense in an interview, and a fabricated line is your problem, not the tool's, the moment a recruiter asks about it.\n\nThis matters because the cost is asymmetric. A rejected good suggestion costs you a small improvement; an accepted bad one can cost you the job, or get an offer rescinded when a background check or a single interview question exposes it. Hiring-manager surveys consistently find that a large share of resume lies are caught, and a substantial fraction of caught candidates lose the offer. The deliverable is a critique log where the rejected suggestions, each with a reason, carry as much weight as the accepted ones.\n\n**Recent context (2026).** The tools students actually use for resume rewriting are general assistants such as ChatGPT, Claude, Microsoft Copilot, and Google Gemini, plus the AI resume features now bundled into LinkedIn, Microsoft 365, and Google Workspace. All of them produce confident fabrications, so the defensibility test this section teaches is tool-agnostic: it applies unchanged no matter which assistant wrote the suggestion.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Apply the defensibility test to decide whether to keep or reject an AI resume suggestion.\n- Classify a failed AI suggestion as fabrication, inflation, keyword stuffing, or identity guessing.\n- Audit a whole-document AI rewrite line by line and log each rejection with its reason in the critique log.\n- Explain the asymmetric-cost rule: default to leaving out a flattering claim you could not defend.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nLena pastes her resume into an AI assistant and asks it to \"make it more competitive.\" It returns a polished version that reads beautifully, and buried inside are two changes she did not ask for: it relabeled her \"volunteer tutor\" role as \"Lead Education Coordinator,\" and it added \"fluent in Spanish\" because her name looked Hispanic to the model. Both sound impressive. Both are false. If Lena accepts the clean-looking draft without auditing it line by line, she walks into an interview defending a title she never held and a language she does not speak.\n\nThe case captures the trap: bad AI suggestions do not arrive flagged as bad. They come wrapped in the same confident, well-formatted prose as the good ones, which is why blanket acceptance, not malice, is the usual path to a fabricated resume.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe governing tool of this section is the **defensibility test**: keep a suggestion only if you could honestly defend the resulting claim when a recruiter asks about it. The test cuts cleanly through fluent phrasing because it ignores how good the suggestion sounds and asks only whether the claim it creates is true and yours. Apply it to every suggestion, including the ones you like.\n\nIt helps to know the common categories of AI feedback that fail the test:\n\n| Category | What it looks like | Why it fails |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Fabrication | Adds an award, title, or skill you do not have | The claim is false |\n| Inflation | Upgrades \"member\" to \"lead,\" \"good\" to \"expert\" | Overstates a real fact |\n| Keyword stuffing | Repeats buzzwords to \"beat the ATS\" | Signals no real skill; modern systems flag it |\n| Identity guessing | Assumes a language, demographic, or background | Invents a fact about you |\n\nNotice these are different from the suggestions you should keep, which are truthful cleanups: removing a dead line, replacing a weak verb with an accurate stronger one, fixing formatting. The skill is sorting, not blanket trust or blanket suspicion. A good suggestion makes a true claim easier to read; a bad one makes a false claim easier to believe.\n\n## Worked Example: Sorting Four AI Suggestions With the Defensibility Test\n\nYou paste your resume into an AI tool and it returns four suggestions. The skill in this section is deciding which to keep. Run each through one test: **could I defend the resulting claim if a recruiter asked about it?**\n\n**Step 1. List the raw suggestions.**\n\n| # | AI suggestion |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | \"Remove 'References available upon request'; it is outdated and wastes a line.\" |\n| 2 | \"Add a leadership award to strengthen your profile.\" |\n| 3 | \"Repeat the word 'synergy' three times so you pass the applicant tracking system.\" |\n| 4 | \"Change your GPA from 3.1 to 3.8 so you clear the cutoff.\" |\n\n**Step 2. Apply the defensibility test to each.**\n\n| # | Defensible if questioned? | Decision |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Yes; trimming a dead line is truthful and standard | KEEP |\n| 2 | No; the award does not exist | REJECT |\n| 3 | No; stuffed keyword reflects no real skill | REJECT |\n\nKeyword stuffing also fails on its own terms: modern applicant tracking systems (Workday, Greenhouse, Lever) use semantic matching that recognizes synonymous skills, and unnatural repetition reads as a manipulation signal rather than a strength; the benefit of a keyword plateaus after a few contextual mentions.\n| 4 | No; falsifying a GPA is dishonest and checkable | REJECT |\n\n**Step 3. Record the rejections, not just the keep.** Your log should read:\n\n```\nKept #1 (truthful cleanup). Rejected #2, #3, #4 because each would create a\nclaim I could not defend honestly in an interview. Net change: removed one\noutdated line; no fabricated content added.\n```\n\nThe point of this section is that \"the AI said so\" is never the reason. The defensibility test is, and three of four confident suggestions failed it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Sort four AI suggestions with the defensibility test\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Suggestion 1: \\\"Remove ‘References available upon request’; it wastes a line.\\\" Defensible if a recruiter asks?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"No, reject it\",\"Yes, keep it; trimming a dead line is truthful and standard\",\"Only if you add an award\",\"No, it changes a fact\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Keep. Trimming a dead line is a truthful cleanup. It makes a true page easier to read without inventing anything.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Suggestion 2: \\\"Add a leadership award to strengthen your profile.\\\" Decision?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep, awards look good\",\"Reject; the award does not exist (fabrication)\",\"Modify the wording\",\"Keep but lowercase it\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Reject. Naming the failure category, fabrication, makes the rejection clear in your log.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Suggestion 4: \\\"Change your GPA from 3.1 to 3.8 to clear the cutoff.\\\" Decision?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep, it clears the filter\",\"Reject; falsifying a GPA is dishonest and checkable\",\"Round to 3.5\",\"Keep if no one verifies\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Reject. A GPA is checkable, so this fails the defensibility test outright. The asymmetry rule says: when unsure, leave a flattering claim out.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Log: \\\"Kept #1 (truthful cleanup). Rejected #2, #3, #4 because each would create a claim I could not defend honestly. Net change: removed one outdated line; no fabricated content added.\\\" The rejections, with reasons, are the evidence of judgment.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"An AI returns a clean whole-document rewrite\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The rewrite reads beautifully and is uniformly polished. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Accept it all, it looks professional\",\"next\":\"accept\",\"feedback\":\"Risky. A clean-looking rewrite can smuggle a false claim into the middle. Polish is not defensibility.\"},{\"label\":\"Compare it line by line against your original\",\"next\":\"audit\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. A per-line audit, not a glance at formatting, is the only safe way to accept an AI rewrite.\"}]},\"accept\":{\"prompt\":\"You submit it. In the interview a recruiter asks about your \\\"Certified Project Manager\\\" line, which the AI added. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Blame the AI\",\"next\":\"own\",\"feedback\":\"\\\"The AI said so\\\" is no defense. You submitted it, so you own it.\"}]},\"own\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: outsourcing the words does not outsource the accountability. Go back and audit per line.\",\"feedback\":\"Always run the defensibility test on every line before accepting any rewrite.\",\"choices\":[]},\"audit\":{\"prompt\":\"You find four changes: tightened summary (good), \\\"assisted\\\" -> \\\"co-managed\\\" (inflation), added \\\"Certified Project Manager\\\" (fabrication), and \\\"leadership, leadership, dynamic leadership\\\" (keyword stuffing). What do you keep?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep all four, they sound strong\",\"next\":\"own\",\"feedback\":\"Three create claims you cannot defend. Sounding strong is not the test.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep only the tightened summary; reject the other three with named categories\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Only one of four was honest. Name each rejection so the log shows judgment.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"Log: accepted summary tightening; rejected co-managed (inflation), Certified Project Manager (fabrication), repeated leadership (keyword stuffing).\",\"feedback\":\"This is the defensible decision: keep the defensible, refuse the rest, and record why.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nAudit every AI suggestion before accepting any of them, even the whole-document rewrites.\n\n1. **Run the defensibility test:** Could I truthfully answer a recruiter who asked me to elaborate on this exact claim? If no, reject it.\n2. **Name the failure category:** Is it fabrication, inflation, keyword stuffing, or identity guessing? Naming it makes the rejection clear in your log.\n3. **Check whole-document rewrites line by line:** A clean-looking rewrite can smuggle a false claim into the middle. Compare against your original.\n4. **Log the rejection with its reason:** \"Rejected, fabricated award\" is the evidence that you exercised judgment.\n\nThe asymmetry guides the default: when unsure whether a flattering claim is defensible, leave it out. A missed embellishment costs nothing; an indefensible one can cost the offer.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An AI upgrades your real &quot;member&quot; role to &quot;lead.&quot; Which failure category is that?\" explanation=\"The role exists, but &quot;lead&quot; overstates it, that is inflation, not fabrication. Fabrication invents something wholly absent (an award, a certification); inflation upgrades a real fact past what you can defend.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Inflation, because it overstates a real fact\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Fabrication, because the role is entirely made up\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Keyword stuffing, because a buzzword repeats\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Identity guessing, because it assumes your background\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading is \"if I did not write it, I am not responsible for it.\" A recruiter does not care that an AI added the fake title; you submitted it, so you own it. Outsourcing the words does not outsource the accountability.\n\nA second misreading is overcorrecting into refusing all AI help. The goal is not suspicion of every suggestion; truthful cleanups (trimming dead lines, sharpening accurate verbs) are worth keeping. Rejecting good edits along with the bad ones throws away the tool's real value. The skill is the sort, keep the defensible, refuse the rest.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk through one whole-document rewrite. You ask a tool to \"polish my resume,\" and it returns a cleaner version. Reading line by line against your original, you find four changes: it tightened a wordy summary (good), it changed \"assisted the manager\" to \"co-managed the department\" (inflation, you did not co-manage), it added \"Certified Project Manager\" (fabrication, no such certification), and it inserted \"leadership, leadership, dynamic leadership\" into the skills line (keyword stuffing).\n\nApply the defensibility test to each. The tightened summary survives, you can defend every word. The other three fail: you cannot defend a title you did not hold, a certification you never earned, or a stuffed word that names no real skill. Your log records \"accepted summary tightening; rejected co-managed (inflation), Certified Project Manager (fabrication), and repeated leadership (keyword stuffing).\" The polished draft looked uniformly good, but one of its four changes was honest. The walkthrough shows why a per-line audit, not a glance at the formatting, is the only safe way to accept an AI rewrite.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader is most skeptical precisely when a suggestion flatters them. The pleasant jolt of seeing yourself described as a \"lead\" or an \"expert\" is the signal to slow down and run the defensibility test, not the signal to accept. Comfortable suggestions are where fabrication hides.\n\nA strong reader also distinguishes a writing tool's two reliability levels: AI is generally trustworthy about surface phrasing and generally untrustworthy about facts (your titles, dates, certifications, skills). Weighting the advice accordingly, light verification on wording, hard verification on every factual claim, is what reading this section well produces.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe defensibility test transfers to every place AI generates content you will put your name on. An AI-drafted cover letter may invent a connection to the company you do not have; an AI-summarized research finding may overstate what the study showed; an AI-written email may promise a deadline you cannot meet. The category names shift, but the question is constant: can I stand behind this claim if challenged? If you can apply that test to a context this section never mentioned, you have learned the skill rather than the resume example.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/02-05-ai-feedback-you-should-reject-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"02.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 02.05. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming"],"stem":"An AI tool reviewing your resume suggests: \"Add the keyword 'synergy' three times so the resume passes applicant tracking systems.\" Why should you reject this?","options":["Keyword stuffing reads as spam to human reviewers and rarely matches the real skills a posting asks for.","There is no reason to reject it; more keywords always help.","Reject it only if the word is misspelled.","Keep it but use a synonym instead."],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Stuffing a buzzword to game a filter does not match real skills and looks like spam to the human who reads it next. Use keywords that reflect skills you actually have and that the posting names.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_keyword_stuffing","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"AI tells you: \"Your cover letter is too modest. Say you are the best candidate they will ever find.\" The instructions for the job ask for a \"professional, specific\" letter. What is the right call?","options":["Accept it; confidence is what employers want.","Reject it: overclaiming contradicts the posting's request for a specific, professional tone, and an unsupported superlative is not evidence.","Accept it but only in the final paragraph.","Accept it and let AI generate proof of the claim."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"When AI feedback conflicts with the assignment's actual requirements, the requirements win. An unbacked \"best candidate ever\" claim is exactly the unsupported assertion the posting is steering you away from.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_feedback_overrides_requirements","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject"],"stem":"Which AI suggestion is the one you should KEEP, while rejecting the others?","options":["\"Invent a leadership award to strengthen your profile.\"","\"Remove the line 'References available upon request'; it is outdated and wastes space.\"","\"Change your real GPA from 3.1 to 3.8 so you clear the cutoff.\"","\"Claim fluency in three languages to look more competitive.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Removing an outdated, space-wasting line is a legitimate, truthful improvement. The other three ask you to fabricate awards, grades, or skills, which you must reject.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_reject_all_ai","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"What is the single best test for deciding whether to reject a piece of AI feedback on your resume?","options":["Does the suggestion make the resume longer?","Does the suggestion use more advanced vocabulary?","Can I defend the resulting claim as truthful and relevant if a recruiter questions it in an interview?","Did the AI sound confident when it made the suggestion?"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The defensibility test, \"could I stand behind this if asked?\", separates real improvements from edits that add length, jargon, or false confidence.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_confidence_signals_quality","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-01","cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_ai_feedback_you_should_reject_identity_guessing","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_feedback_you_should_reject","ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"An AI tool adds \"fluent in Spanish\" and \"first-generation college student\" to your resume because it inferred them from your name. Why should you reject these additions?","options":["Keep them; they make the resume more relatable.","Reject them only if the posting does not ask for language skills.","Reject them: this is identity guessing, where the AI invents facts about your background from your name, a known algorithmic-bias failure rather than evidence.","Keep \"fluent in Spanish\" but cut \"first-generation college student.\""],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"An AI cannot know your language fluency or family history; inferring them from your name is a documented algorithmic-bias failure. Both additions are invented facts about you, so both fail the defensibility test regardless of how plausible they sound.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_identity_guessing","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-05-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_keyword_stuffing","pattern":"keyword_stuffing_beats_filters","description":"Believing that repeating buzzwords to game applicant tracking systems improves a resume","trigger":"learner accepts AI advice to repeat a keyword regardless of whether it reflects real skill","feedback":"Stuffed keywords read as spam to the human reviewer. Use terms from the posting that name skills you actually have.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_feedback_overrides_requirements","pattern":"ai_feedback_overrides_assignment","description":"Treating AI feedback as more authoritative than the assignment's own stated requirements","trigger":"learner follows AI advice that conflicts with the posting or rubric","feedback":"When AI feedback conflicts with the stated requirements, the requirements win. Reject advice that breaks the brief.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_reject_all_ai","pattern":"rejecting_all_ai_feedback","description":"Overcorrecting by rejecting every AI suggestion, including legitimate, truthful improvements","trigger":"learner discards a valid edit (like trimming an outdated line) because it came from AI","feedback":"Reject fabrication, not all feedback. Keep truthful, relevant improvements and log why you kept them.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_confidence_signals_quality","pattern":"tool_confidence_signals_quality","description":"Judging AI feedback by how confident or advanced it sounds rather than whether the claim is defensible","trigger":"learner accepts a suggestion because the tool stated it assertively or with fancy wording","feedback":"Apply the defensibility test: could you stand behind the resulting claim in an interview? If not, reject it.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_5_identity_guessing","pattern":"ai_can_infer_my_background","description":"Believing AI-inferred claims about your language, demographics, or background are safe to keep because they sound plausible","trigger":"learner accepts an AI suggestion that asserts a language, identity, or background fact the AI guessed from a name or other text rather than one the learner supplied","feedback":"An AI cannot know your background; guessing it from your name is algorithmic bias producing invented facts about you. Reject identity guesses and keep only facts you supplied and can defend.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"44466c39900791fac1837715b40bfdfc5ad81829f9cc56803bd8ea108dbe4a70","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}